<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A road crew of <m>{14}</m> workers, working <m>{9}</m> hours a day, takes <m>{8}</m> days to gravel a stretch of road.</p><p>(a) How many worker-hours does the job take?</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> worker-hours  </p><p>(b) How many days would <m>{16}</m> workers take to do the same job, working <m>{7}</m> hours a day? Give your answer exactly or correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> days  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Reduce to one worker working one hour a day.</p><p><m>{14}</m> workers working <m>{9}</m> hours a day take <m>{8}</m> days.<br/>1 worker working <m>{9}</m> hours a day takes <m>{14} \times {8} = {112}</m> days.<br/>1 worker working 1 hour a day takes <m>{9} \times {112} = {1008}</m> days.</p><p>So the job is <m>{14} \times {9} \times {8} = {1008}</m> worker-hours.</p><p>(b) Change one quantity at a time and ask whether the answer gets bigger or smaller. More workers, and more hours a day, both mean fewer days.</p><p><m>{16}</m> workers working <m>{7}</m> hours a day give <m>{16} \times {7} = {112}</m> worker-hours a day.<br/>Days needed <m>= {1008} \div {112} = {9}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1008</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>9</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>